      "level": 2,
      "content": "1. Definition"
    },
    {
      "type": "keyFacts",
      "title": "Key Takeaways",
      "items": [
        "The basic rule of subject-verb agreement requires singular subjects to take singular verbs and plural subjects to take plural verbs.",
        "Subjects joined by 'And' usually take a plural verb unless they express a single idea or refer to the same person/thing.",
        "When subjects are connected by 'Or', 'Nor', 'Either…Or', or 'Neither…Nor', the verb agrees with the subject nearest to it.",
        "Collective nouns take a singular verb when acting as a unified group, and a plural verb when members act individually.",
        "Expressions starting with 'A number of' take a plural verb, whereas 'The number of' takes a singular verb.",
        "Phrases starting with 'Many a', 'Each', and 'Every' strictly require singular nouns and singular verbs."
      ],
      "id": "t13cw6j"
    },
